Stop head when a count from the end is too big.
With -n -N or -c -N, head checked the count against the size of the
array of saved lines but only printed "count is too big" and carried
on. For 4294967295 the ++count in print_except_N_last_lines and
print_except_N_last_bytes wrapped to zero, and on 32-bit hosts
(count + 1) * sizeof(char*) wrapped for line counts of 2^30 - 1 and
above, so the ring buffer was undersized and written past its end.
Where long is 64 bits, a count above 4294967295 was also truncated
when passed on as unsigned.

Make the error fatal, as intended. The limit for lines stays the one
that keeps the array size in range; bytes need only one byte each, so
allow them up to INT_MAX rather than refusing counts that worked.

---
 coreutils/head.c |    4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/coreutils/head.c
+++ b/coreutils/head.c
@@ -235,8 +235,8 @@
 #endif
 	}
 	if (negative_N) {
-		if (count >= INT_MAX / sizeof(char*))
-			bb_error_msg("count is too big: %lu", count);
+		if (count >= (count_bytes ? INT_MAX : INT_MAX / sizeof(char*)))
+			bb_error_msg_and_die("count is too big: %lu", count);
 	}
 
 	do {
